 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Instability:** Directly training with a quantized bottleneck (CodecSplat) is unstable; the two-stage separation is required to stabilize Gaussian generation [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Parameter Freezing:** In GS-IR, failing to freeze geometry and appearance in Stage 2 can lead to suboptimal material decomposition [S2].
-*   **Depth Normalization:** In CodecSplat, the normalization of inverse depth bounds ($n_i, f_i$) must be consistent between encoder and decoder to ensure $D_{codec-depth}$ is meaningful [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Instability:** Directly training with quantized bottlenecks is unstable; the two-stage separation is required to prevent gradient conflicts between generation and coding [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Precision:** Normalized inverse depth calculation requires careful handling of near/far plane bounds ($n_i, f_i$) to avoid division by zero or numerical instability [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Resolution Mismatch:** In volumetric approaches, failing to switch from low-res to full-res sampling in Stage 2 results in loss of fine details [S1].
 
